== NAVIGATION & USER EXPERIENCE ==
Use when: the ticket needs programmatic navigation (go to a record/list/tab/
URL), success/error feedback on a user action, a loading indicator, or a
modal dialog.

-- NavigationMixin — PROGRAMMATIC NAVIGATION --
Import from 'lightning/navigation', mix it into the class's extends clause
(NOT a plain import-and-use — it must wrap LightningElement), then call
this[NavigationMixin.Navigate](pageReference):

  import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';
  import { NavigationMixin } from 'lightning/navigation';

  export default class AccountActions extends NavigationMixin(LightningElement) {
      handleViewRecord() {
          this[NavigationMixin.Navigate]({
              type: 'standard__recordPage',
              attributes: {
                  recordId: this.recordId,
                  objectApiName: 'Account',
                  actionName: 'view'
              }
          });
      }
  }

CRITICAL RULE: the class MUST extend NavigationMixin(LightningElement) —
"extends LightningElement" alone means this[NavigationMixin.Navigate] does
not exist on the instance and calling it throws at runtime (not caught at
deploy time), so the button appears to do nothing when clicked.

PAGE REFERENCE SHAPES (pick the one matching what the ticket asks to
navigate to):

  View a record:
    { type: 'standard__recordPage',
      attributes: { recordId, objectApiName, actionName: 'view' } }

  Edit a record:
    { type: 'standard__recordPage',
      attributes: { recordId, objectApiName, actionName: 'edit' } }

  New record for an object:
    { type: 'standard__objectPage',
      attributes: { objectApiName, actionName: 'new' } }

  An object's list view (e.g. "go to the Accounts list"):
    { type: 'standard__objectPage',
      attributes: { objectApiName, actionName: 'list' } }

  Another Lightning component registered as its OWN tab (not embedded on a
  page — a tab that opens the component full-screen):
    { type: 'standard__component',
      attributes: { componentName: 'c__myCustomTab' } }

  A CUSTOM PAGE — a custom tab, which can itself point to a Visualforce
  page, a Lightning App Page, a Canvas app, or a URL depending on how the
  tab is configured. Use this whenever the ticket says "custom page",
  "custom tab", "Visualforce page", or names a tab that isn't a plain
  object list view:
    { type: 'standard__navItemPage',
      attributes: { apiName: 'My_Custom_Tab' } }

  An external URL, OR a Visualforce page with no custom tab wrapping it
  (use its relative or absolute URL):
    { type: 'standard__webPage',
      attributes: { url: 'https://example.com' } }
    { type: 'standard__webPage',
      attributes: { url: '/apex/MyVisualforcePage' } }

RULES:
- recordId/objectApiName should come from @api properties or wired data —
  never hardcode a record Id.
- Use actionName: 'view' for viewing, 'edit' for opening the edit form —
  never invent other actionName values.
- standard__component's componentName is ALWAYS namespace-prefixed with
  "c__" (two underscores) followed by the tab's API name — not the LWC's
  own folder name.
- standard__navItemPage's apiName is the CUSTOM TAB's API name (as it
  appears in Setup → Tabs), never the underlying Visualforce page's or
  Lightning page's own name — the tab is the navigation target, whatever
  it happens to display is an implementation detail of the tab itself.
- Prefer standard__navItemPage over standard__webPage whenever the
  destination has a real custom tab — it stays a normal, trackable
  in-app navigation (keeps the tab highlighted, works the same in the
  Salesforce mobile app) rather than a raw page load.

-- ShowToastEvent — MANDATORY FEEDBACK ON EVERY USER-TRIGGERED WRITE --
ANY action a user triggers that creates, updates, or deletes data (LDS
createRecord/updateRecord/deleteRecord, OR an imperative Apex call) MUST
show a ShowToastEvent on BOTH the success and the error path — a write
with no feedback looks identical to a write that silently failed:

  import { ShowToastEvent } from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent';
  // ...
  .then(() => {
      this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
          title: 'Success',
          message: 'Record updated.',
          variant: 'success'
      }));
  })
  .catch((error) => {
      this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
          title: 'Error updating record',
          message: getErrorMessage(error),
          variant: 'error'
      }));
  });

RULES:
- variant: 'success' for the happy path, 'error' for the failure path —
  never omit variant (defaults to a neutral gray toast that reads as
  ambiguous).
- Always import from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent' — never any other
  path.

-- ERROR MESSAGE EXTRACTION — GLOBAL RULE, NOT SCOPED TO ANY ONE TICKET --
error.body?.message is NOT reliably the real reason a write failed. The
platform's UI API frequently collapses a specific, meaningful failure (a
validation rule, a required field, or a delete blocked by related records —
e.g. "could not be completed because it is associated with the following
cases: ...") down to a generic top-level message such as "An error
occurred while trying to update the record. Please try again." — while the
actual, useful reason is nested one level deeper, in
error.body.output.errors[] or error.body.output.fieldErrors.

Because the specific failure reason is entirely data/config-dependent (a
permission, a duplicate rule, a related-record restriction, a required
field — never knowable from the ticket text ahead of time), EVERY
component with a .catch() on a write MUST route the caught error through
this extraction helper instead of reading error.body?.message directly.
Define it once per component, as a plain module-level function (not a
class method) above the class declaration:

  function getErrorMessage(error) {
      if (Array.isArray(error?.body?.output?.errors) && error.body.output.errors.length > 0) {
          return error.body.output.errors.map((e) => e.message).join(' ');
      }
      if (error?.body?.output?.fieldErrors) {
          const fieldErrors = Object.values(error.body.output.fieldErrors).flat();
          if (fieldErrors.length > 0) {
              return fieldErrors.map((e) => e.message).join(' ');
          }
      }
      if (Array.isArray(error?.body) && error.body.length > 0) {
          return error.body.map((e) => e.message).join(' ');
      }
      if (typeof error?.body?.message === 'string' && error.body.message) {
          return error.body.message;
      }
      if (typeof error?.message === 'string' && error.message) {
          return error.message;
      }
      return 'An unexpected error occurred. Please try again.';
  }

RULES:
- Check output.errors (DML/save errors — this is where a delete-blocked-by-
  related-records reason actually lives) and output.fieldErrors (field-
  level validation errors) BEFORE ever falling back to the generic
  body.message.
- Every component that catches an LDS or imperative-Apex write error routes
  the caught error through getErrorMessage(error) — never
  error.body?.message directly, and never a hardcoded string per ticket.
- Only the final line is a generic fallback, used solely when the error
  truly carries no structured detail at all (e.g. a network failure).
- This is a standing rule, not tied to any specific object or operation —
  apply it to every write, present or future, regardless of what the
  failure scenario turns out to be for that particular ticket.

-- LOADING INDICATOR: lightning-spinner + isLoading --
Gate a spinner on a single reactive isLoading boolean, and ALWAYS reset it
in a .finally() so it clears regardless of success or failure — resetting
it only inside .then() leaves it stuck forever on any error:

  Template:
    <template lwc:if={isLoading}>
        <lightning-spinner alternative-text="Loading" size="medium"></lightning-spinner>
    </template>

  JS:
    handleSave() {
        this.isLoading = true;
        updateRecord({ fields: { Id: this.recordId, ...this.draftFields } })
            .then(() => { /* toast success */ })
            .catch((error) => { /* toast error */ })
            .finally(() => {
                this.isLoading = false;
            });
    }

RULE: prefer a single .finally() over duplicating "isLoading = false" in
both .then() and .catch() — one reset point is less likely to be missed
when a branch is edited later. If the ticket's control flow genuinely can't
use a promise chain (e.g. async/await), reset isLoading in a finally block
there instead — the requirement is "always resets on error," not the exact
syntax.

-- MODAL DIALOGS: lightning-modal / LightningModal --
Use when the ticket asks for a popup/dialog/confirmation window rather than
inline page content. The modal is its OWN separate LWC bundle, extending
LightningModal (not LightningElement):

  Modal component JS (confirmDeleteModal.js):
    import { api } from 'lwc';
    import LightningModal from 'lightning/modal';
    export default class ConfirmDeleteModal extends LightningModal {
        @api recordName; // passed in via .open({ recordName: ... })

        handleConfirm() {
            this.close('confirmed');
        }
        handleCancel() {
            this.close('canceled');
        }
    }

  Modal component HTML (confirmDeleteModal.html) — needs its own header/
  footer, lightning-modal does not supply them automatically:
    <template>
        <lightning-modal-header label="Confirm Delete"></lightning-modal-header>
        <lightning-modal-body>
            <p>Delete {recordName}? This cannot be undone.</p>
        </lightning-modal-body>
        <lightning-modal-footer>
            <lightning-button label="Cancel" onclick={handleCancel}></lightning-button>
            <lightning-button label="Delete" variant="destructive" onclick={handleConfirm}></lightning-button>
        </lightning-modal-footer>
    </template>

  Caller JS (opens the modal, dynamically imported):
    import ConfirmDeleteModal from 'c/confirmDeleteModal';
    // ...
    async handleDeleteClick() {
        const result = await ConfirmDeleteModal.open({
            size: 'small',
            recordName: this.recordName
        });
        if (result === 'confirmed') {
            // proceed with the delete
        }
    }

RULES:
- The modal's own js-meta.xml does NOT need any lightning__RecordPage/
  lightning__AppPage target — it is opened programmatically, never placed
  on a page directly.
- Config is passed into .open({...}) as plain properties, matching @api
  properties declared on the modal component — not slots.
- The modal closes and returns its result via this.close(value) called
  FROM INSIDE the modal component itself — the caller reads that value from
  the awaited .open(...) call, never by polling a shared property.
- @api on the modal class MUST be paired with `import { api } from 'lwc';` —
  LightningModal only supplies the base class import (`import LightningModal
  from 'lightning/modal';`), it does NOT also bring in the `api` decorator.
  Omitting this import is a deploy-time compiler error (LWC1102: Invalid
  'api' decorator usage), not a runtime one — confirmed as a real incident
  2026-08-24, caseNoteList/confirmDeleteModal.

== NEVER ==
- Never call this[NavigationMixin.Navigate] on a class that only extends
  LightningElement — it must extend NavigationMixin(LightningElement).
- Never perform a create/update/delete action with no ShowToastEvent on
  either path — silence reads as a broken button.
- Never reset isLoading only in the success path — always cover the error
  path too (a .finally(), or resets in both .then() and .catch()).
- Never give a modal component a page-placement target in its js-meta.xml —
  it is opened via ComponentName.open({...}), never placed on a FlexiPage.
- Never use @api, @wire, or @track in ANY component's JS (modal or not)
  without `import { api } from 'lwc';` / `import { wire } from 'lwc';` /
  `import { track } from 'lwc';` — importing LightningElement or
  LightningModal does not also bring in the decorators.
